    </track></source></video></div>
</div>


<title>Try It: Solving Equations and Creating Equivalent Equations</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write two equivalent equations to \( 2(x+4)= 12 \).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Solution:</emphasis></para>
<para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
<list>
  <item>\(2x + 8 = 24 \)</item>
  <item>\(x + 4 = 6 \)</item>
  <item>\(2x = 16\)
  </item>
</list>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Solve \(2(x+4)=12\).</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work. </para>
<para><emphasis>Solution:</emphasis></para>
<para>Compare your work: Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
<para>
<emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation  as much  as possible. Use the  Distributive  Property to remove any parentheses.</para>
<para>  \(\begin{array}{rcl}2(x + 4) &amp;=&amp; 12 \\ 
2x + 8 &amp;= &amp;12\end{array}\)</para>
<para>  <emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable  terms on one side of the  equation. Nothing  to do. All the variables  of x are on the left side.</para>
<para>  <emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Collect all the  constant terms on the other side of the equation.</para>
<para>Use the  Addition or Subtraction Property of Equality.</para>
<para>  \(\begin{array}{rcl}2x + 8{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}8} &amp;=&amp; 12{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}8 }\\ 
2x &amp;=&amp; 4\end{array}\)</para>
<para>  <emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Make the coefficient of the variable  term equal to 1. Use the Multiplication or Division Property of Equality.</para>
<para>  \(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{2x}{2} &amp;=&amp; \frac42 \\ 
x &amp;=&amp; 2\end{array}\)</para>
<para>  <emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
<para>Substitute the solution  into the original equation to make sure  the result  is a true statement.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2(x + 4) &amp;=&amp; 12 \\ 
  2({\style{color:red}2} + 4) &amp;=&amp; 12 \\ 
  2(6) &amp;=&amp; 12 \\ 
12 &amp;=&amp; 12 \checkmark\end{array}\)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>
    Solve \(-12+8(x−5)=-4+3(5x-2)    \)</item>
</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the solution button to compare your work. </para>
<para><emphasis>Solution:</emphasis></para>
<para>Compare your work: </para>

<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Simplify each side of the equation as much as possible.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-12 + 8(x - 5) &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(5x - 2) \\ -12 + 8x - 40 &amp;=&amp; -4 + 15x - 6 \\ 8x - 52 &amp;=&amp; 15x - 10\end{array}\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Collect all the variable terms on one side of the equation.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}8x - 52 - 15x &amp;=&amp; 15x - 10 - 15x \\ -7x -52 &amp;=&amp; - 10\end{array}\)</para>

<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Collect all the constant terms on the other side of the equation.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-7x - 52 + 52 &amp;=&amp; -10 + 52 \\ -7x &amp;=&amp; 42\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Make the coefficient of the variable term equal to 1.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}\frac{-7x}{-7} &amp;=&amp; \frac {42}{-7} \\ x &amp;=&amp; -6\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5 -</emphasis> Check the solution.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-12 + 8(x - 5) &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(5x - 2) ? \\ -12 + 8(-6 - 5) &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(5(-6) - 2) ? \\ -12 + 8(-11) &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(-30 - 2) ? \\ -12 + -88 &amp;=&amp; -4 + 3(-32) ? \\ -100 &amp;=&amp; -4 + -96 \\ -100 &amp;=&amp; -100  \checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
</section>
